WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.060
For all its gross-out gags that have made
it a favorite for the kids, there's a whole

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.720
lot for grown-up viewers to enjoy in Teen
Titans Go!

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.790
From the Filmation references to the 1980s
power ballads, here are the things only adults

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.430
notice about this crazy DC show.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:15.460
Teen Titans Go!

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.740
To the Movies focuses on everyone's favorite
team of sidekicks, but it's a delight for

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.580
any fan who loves the larger universe of DC
Comics.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.760
That might come as a surprise, though, when
you consider the cartoon's well-deserved reputation

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.340
for absurdity.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.579
For the most part, that's a rep that the Titans
have earned it's not exactly the intense drama

00:00:31.579 --> 00:00:36.690
of Batman: The Animated Series but that doesn't
mean a diehard fan won't love all the references,

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.739
in-jokes, and tributes crammed into their
cinematic adventure's 90-minute running time.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:45.710
In fact, one of the most charming pieces of
the film is also the fulfillment of one particularly

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.320
dedicated DC fan's long-held desire.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.700
That fan is Nicolas Cage, who finally got
the chance to play Superman on the big screen.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:55.510
Now, Cage isn't your garden-variety superhero
fan.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:01.140
He paid over two million dollars for a pristine
copy of Action Comics #1, the Man of Steel's

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.820
historic first appearance.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.420
Not only did he name his son after Superman,
he skipped over "Clark" and went straight

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.760
to the deep-cut Kryptonian name of Kal-El.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.780
He's about as devoted as any fan can be, which
made the collapse of Superman Lives, the Tim

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.770
Burton film in which he was set to play the
big blue boy scout, all the more devastating.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.300
Twenty years later, however, he finally got
to play the role in Teen Titans Go!

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:22.920
To the Movies.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.980
"Hey, I guess you kids weren’t such goofsters
after all."

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.979
Kids might not realize the behind-the scenes
gag, but that's a union of fan and icon that

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:35.479
every adult who has obsessed over the idea
of Nic Cage fighting a giant robot spider

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:36.160
could appreciate.

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:41.000
It might not be Superman Lives, and there's
sadly no spider to be found, but it sure is

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.000
better than nothing.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.380
Season 3's "Finally a Lesson" isn't quite
like any other episode of Teen Titans Go!

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.580
For one thing, it's actually educational…
maybe a little too educational.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.400
Robin takes the Titans on a whirlwind journey
through the world of property investment,

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:58.890
because it's time the team learned something
practical, and what's more practical than

00:01:58.890 --> 00:01:59.420
real estate?

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.880
Thus the teens, however reluctantly, learn
about down payments, rent control, and most

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.640
importantly of all, building equity.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.080
That last part in particular becomes a lot
more exciting once they learn it's just a

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.590
fancy way of saying "money."

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.880
"Equity is the part of property you actually
own.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.360
It’s the difference between your loan balance
and your properties market value.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.680
If you sold your property and paid off the
bank the value of your equity..."

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.220
The point of the episode that it's boring
and adult-centric isn't lost on kids, who

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.900
get that Robin is a stuffy dweeb enforcing
a hilariously inappropriate tone upon the

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:29.400
show.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:33.540
But what they can't grasp is that Robin is
actually giving out some pretty good advice.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.960
"Finally a Lesson" is, in fact, a great introduction
to the rental market, with solid tips on how

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.600
to improve your credit score and how to save
for retirement.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.980
Sure, the airhorns that blare when Robin celebrates
all that equity finally funding "Old People

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.980
Medication!" are sarcastic, but darn it if
watchers over 30 won't be nodding along.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.840
Decades later, perhaps some of the show's
youngest fans will look back and realize that

00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:57.290
Robin's lessons are the reason they became
realtors.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.630
Or maybe they'll just feel weird when they
remember that the Teen Titans once bought

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.570
an apartment building and immediately evicted
everyone to make more money.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:09.489
For a cartoon aimed at grade-schoolers, Teen
Titans Go! spends quite a bit of time on romance.

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:13.930
Generally speaking, it keeps things fairly
tame, but there were entire episodes are devoted

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.430
to Robin wanting to see Starfire in a bikini,
Beast Boy drooling over Raven's legs, and

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.440
a whole lot Looney Toons-style wolf-whistling.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:25.680
Adult eyebrows might raise at these PG-13-ish
moments, but really, that sort of borderline-risque

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:27.959
content has a long history in kids cartoons.

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:30.549
But every so often, Teen Titans Go! steps
over the line.

00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:34.519
Witness the scene from the first season episode
"Real Magic," in which Beast Boy waits on

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:39.049
Raven's bed, clad only in his underwear, having
set the mood with candles and a scatter of

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:39.780
rose petals.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.500
She's not interested, but the implications
of the moment are clear and pretty adult.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.240
Kids might not grasp the full meaning of the
scene, but in the minds of many adults, it's

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:49.659
better not to test that theory.

00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:53.129
In fact, UK fans have reported that the shot
was cut from their broadcast.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:59.220
Teen Titans Go! made its mission clear from
the very first episode: silliness to the extreme,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.540
with a generous dollop of outright bad taste.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.540
Butt jokes, bad puns, extensive mocking of
Batman it's all fair game.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:10.660
Many viewers, especially kids, love it for
exactly those reasons, but there have always

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.800
been a handful of folks who can't stand its
goofy approach to one of DC's most beloved

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.720
superteams, especially the fans of the slightly
darker 2003 series.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.940
"You are a mockery of everything the world
holds sacred about heroes"

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.520
"Come on, what is wrong with being a little
silly from time to time?"

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.190
While younger viewers might not realize this,
Teen Titans Go!

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:33.590
Itself has addressed those critics directly
in what have informally become known as the

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:34.460
"hater episodes."

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.540
These episodes cast the ultra-nerd villain
Control Freak as an aggrieved fan of the Titans'

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.830
previous TV incarnation, and serve as a direct
response to the most vocally displeased viewers.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:49.270
In "The Fourth Wall," Control Freak even plays
clips from the 2003 cartoon to compare the

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.320
current team unfavorably against, only to
have his fandom roundly rebuffed.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:58.640
Still, he remains undaunted, appearing again
and again to taunt the Titans with deleted

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:03.850
scenes, old-school Superfriends style sequences,
and an endless supply of complaints, nitpicks,

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:04.580
and rants.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:09.320
He is, in short, the sort of commentator who
has been talking trash about the series from

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:09.840
day one.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.640
And the show misses no opportunity to make
it clear, through him, how they feel about

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.180
that segment of Titans fandom.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.140
Slade, known in the comics by the over-the-top
name of Deathstroke the Terminator, was a

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:22.960
long time coming to Teen Titans Go!

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:27.570
He was one of the major menaces of the team's
adventures in comics back in the '80s, the

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:32.710
most prominent villain in the 2003 cartoon,
and an increasingly major part of the DC universe

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:33.360
as a whole.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.840
Finally, he returned to face his animated
arch-enemies in Teen Titans Go!

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.220
To the Movies ... only to be mistaken for
Deadpool.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.440
"Deadpool?

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:43.520
What?

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:44.100
I’m not Deadpool.”

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.060
“I thought Deadpool was a good guy.”

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:47.580
“Why does everybody think I'm Deadpool?"

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.190
Since Deadpool's movie carried a hard R rating,
this gag was already meant more for adults

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:52.880
in the audience.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.900
But there's a whole other level of reference
to this joke only the most seasoned of Titans

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.380
fans grasped: Deadpool and Deathstroke bear
some pretty striking similarities, but it's

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.720
actually Marvel's version that's ripping off
Slade.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:13.020
In fact, after seeing Rob Liefeld's designs,
Deadpool's co-creator, Fabian Nicieza, gave their new character
the name Wade Wilson, an overt joke to Deathstroke's

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:13.900
name, Slade Wilson.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.750
None of that matters to the Titans or their
younger fans, of course, but to the savvy

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:20.520
adults in the audience, it's a joke decades
in the making.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:26.860
The episode "40%, 40%, 20%" unleashed an awesome
force upon the Teen Titans Go! universe.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.080
That's right, we're talking about Cyborg's
ultimate pump-up jam, "The Night Begins to

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:31.580
Shine."

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:36.740
A smooth, synthy ode to, uh, whatever "the
night beginning to shine" means, its effect

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:40.100
upon Cyborg is so potent as to have become
a bit of a crutch.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.320
But who could blame him?

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:46.060
When exposed to its sheer '80s-inspired power,
the Titans transform into radical versions

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:51.260
of themselves, with gravity-defying hair,
artfully ripped costumes, and sweet motorcycles.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.120
Transported to a neon desert, they cruise,
lost in the sweet riffs that Cyborg's treasured

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:57.200
cassette tape brings them.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.530
So powerful is this sequence that it not only
transformed Cyborg into a not-so-subtle tribute

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:05.880
to Optimus Prime, it spawned an epic four-episode
arc.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:10.120
It was called "The Day the Night Stopped Beginning
to Shine and Became Dark Even Though It Was

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:10.620
the Day."

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:12.560
It's exactly as righteous as you're imagining.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.940
To a kid, this is all extremely cool you don't
have to be born in the '80s to understand

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.840
that heavy metal dragons are awesome, after
all.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:23.220
For the adults in the audience, though, there's
another layer in the form of the show's spot-on

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.670
parody of 1980s sci-fi, down to the smallest,
most radical detail.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:32.090
The Titans' 80s transformations are straight
out of Mad Max and Voltron, their world like

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:35.910
a fever dream from the pages of Heavy Metal,
with all of it sprinkled with glitter straight

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.510
out of Jem and the Holograms.

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.300
What makes it even more hilarious for older
viewers is that the song isn't actually from

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:41.960
the '80s.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:47.120
Instead, it was written and recorded in 2005
as an intentional throwback for Warner Brothers'

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.280
in-house music library.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.690
When an episode in Season 2 came up a little
short, the show's director threw in a ten-second

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:55.200
clip of Cyborg singing along to the epic jams.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:59.500
Fans wound up loving it, and the rest was
history and a surprise appearance for "The

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:03.250
Night Begins To Shine" on the Billboard charts,
12 years after it was recorded.

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:07.971
With this irresistible groove, Teen Titans
Go! doesn't just poke fun at the decade that

00:08:07.971 --> 00:08:09.210
Cyborg seems to love so much.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:14.500
It celebrates it by bringing its best ideas
to the fore and wrapping them up in an absolute

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:15.500
banger of a song.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.100
Batman isn't exactly a fearsome force of justice
in Teen Titans Go!.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:25.260
He spends most of his time giggling with Commissioner
Gordon, serving as a celebrity judge on talent

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.180
search TV shows, and rocking out on electric
guitar.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.300
Not quite the Dark Knight of legend, but that's
how Teen Titans Go! rolls, right?

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:36.840
No one is as sleek, cool, and powerful within
its cartoon confines as they are in other

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:37.480
DC media.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.840
Batman might be the world's greatest detective
and the hero Gotham needs elsewhere, but in

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:45.260
Teen Titans Go!, he's Robin's weirdo, manchild
pseudo-dad.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.500
Except when it comes to background gags.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:51.560
In these, Batman's corner of the universe
is just as brutal and ruthless as it ever

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:54.600
has been, but only the sharp-eyed grown up
is likely to catch it.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.220
Consider "Sidekick," an episode that sends
the Titans to Gotham City.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:02.450
The scenes in the Batcave contain a massive
number of references to more serious Batman

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:06.090
stories lurking in the background, but nothing
comes close to what's on the middle shelf

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:07.590
of Batman's display case.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:12.610
Next to a photo of Batman and Robin, there
stands a crowbar and an urn ... labeled "ROBIN

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:13.100
II."

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:18.900
This is a reference to the death of the second
Robin, Jason Todd, in the infamous 1988 storyline

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:19.900
"A Death in the Family."

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:24.150
It's a blink-and-you-miss-it moment, but boy
does it add a little darkness to the fun,

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.840
bright world of Teen Titans Go! for those
who manage to catch it.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:31.090
Teen Titans Go! might have started out life
as a TV cartoon, but it still takes a few

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:33.100
shots at DC's cinematic ventures.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.320
In fact, there might be no greater source
of teasing for the DC films, especially since

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:40.770
the show made it into theaters in Teen Titans
Go!

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:41.560
To the Movies.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.260
To begin with, there's the very premise of
the film itself.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:48.760
In the age of the omnipresent superhero movie,
Robin wants one to call his own.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.580
Everyone else has one, so why can't he?

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:51.700
Superman has had plenty.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.420
Wonder Woman admits hers took a while, but
it's been made.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.260
Green Lantern even gets to join in, although
he acknowledges that his team's cinematic

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:00.500
outing wasn't quite as successful as the others.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:04.320
"There was a Green Lantern movie, but we,
we don’t talk about that."

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:09.000
Parodies of cinematic Batman branding over
the years fill the screen, from the bright

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:13.390
yellow of the Tim Burton era to the brushed-steel
aesthetic of the Dark Knight trilogy.

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:18.330
But nothing comes close to topping the Batman
v Superman joke that sees Batman and Superman

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.860
growling at each other about their "mommy's
name."

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:25.520
Teen Titans Go! might love the DC universe,
but that doesn't mean it won't roast its movies.

